# Fingerprint Identity — The Model Above The Sensor [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

> The layer above the sensor is a MODEL: eight entities, one seam, two storage tiers, one
> sync mode, two vocabularies. Identity of a template set is a uuid and completeness is a
> count — there is no hashing anywhere in this layer, by ruling. The sensor knows only
> hop 1 of the match chain and must never acquire a concept it lacks.

---

## The seam

### [CXC-FPID-SEAM] The seam is the template

- rule: the template is an opaque vendor-specific blob, produced by a vendor ceremony, held in a vendor store. Nothing above the seam may look inside one, and nothing above the seam may name a vendor, an opcode, a slot count or a byte length.
- what-this-buys: the entities, the cast, the vocabularies and the match chain outlive a vendor swap. Only the HAL realization and the enrolled bytes do not.
- the-tell: any id, property key, namespace literal or schema field above this seam that names a vendor or a device constant.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-TEMPLATE-PORTABILITY] A vendor swap re-enrolls everyone

- claim: template bytes are a vendor's feature extraction and are not portable between vendors. Swapping sensors means re-enrolling every person.
- what-survives-a-swap: the people, castings, roles, capabilities and the device registry — the whole model, untouched.
- why-stated: a property discovered mid-swap reads as a design failure; stated in the spec it reads as the cost of the swap, which is what it is.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

---

## Entities

### [CXC-FPID-ENTITY-PERSON] Person

- owns: the biometric templates. A template belongs to a person and to nothing else.
- lifetime: stable across campaigns; a person outlives every character they play.
- identifier: person_id, allocated by fingerprint_storage.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-ENTITY-CHARACTER] Character

- owns: the game identity and its link to a role. The XO persists whoever plays it.
- not-owned: no biometric data of any kind. A character has no finger.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-ENTITY-ROLE] Role

- owns: the mapping from role to a SET of capabilities. Durable and GLOBAL — there is no module dimension on a role ([CXC-FPID-RBAC-SCOPE]).
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-ENTITY-CAPABILITY] Capability

- owns: a named thing that may be done — authorize nuclear release, jump the ship.
- the-join: roles map to capability sets; each device declares the ONE capability it gates. Capability is where the two meet, which is why it is neither carried in a payload nor duplicated anywhere.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-ENTITY-MODULE] Game module

- owns: which characters are PLAYABLE in this module. Not roles and not capabilities — a module controls availability, never permission.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-ENTITY-CASTING] Casting

- owns: the binding of module and person to character. Session-scoped and mutable — the only entity here expected to change mid-campaign.
- collapse-is-the-design-error: folding casting into person or character forces a re-enrollment on every re-cast. Re-casting changes the casting table and nothing else — no finger is re-scanned and no template moves.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-ENTITY-TEMPLATE] Template

- owns: one enrolled finger's feature data, opaque. It IS the seam — neither above nor below it, and deliberately absent from the sensor-neutral entity list's count of seven.
- lives-in: the service library (all of them) and each device's store (the current cast).
- identified-by: template_ref, a service-private locator — [CXC-FPID-TEMPLATE-REF].
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-ENTITY-DEVICE] Device

- owns: device_id (required — it is the address every upstream event is published under), the one capability this device gates, and an OPTIONAL nullable location. Nothing biometric.
- location-is-an-attribute-never-an-address: location may be CIC or Raptor_Two or simply unset, and unset is legal — a portable device has no fixed location. A consumer wanting a place JOINS the event to this registry; nothing on the wire carries location.
- registry-is-announce-derived: a device declares itself; nobody types a device into a list. A hand-maintained register goes stale silently — the fleet's own measured antipattern.
- announcement-shape: ruled — a dedicated retained announce at boot and on change, carrying the capabilities payload; see [CXC-FPBUS-MSG-DEVICE-ANNOUNCE].
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

---

## The match chain

### [CXC-FPID-MATCH-CHAIN] Five hops from finger to decision

- hop-1: finger to slot — the sensor's only answer, through the HAL.
- hop-2: slot to person — resolved ON THE DEVICE, from the entries of the template set it holds. The device publishes user_detected naming a person, never a slot.
- hop-3: person to character, via the casting for the current module.
- hop-4: character to role.
- hop-5: role to capability set.
- device-decision: the device tests membership of its one gated capability in the resolved set, applies its own policy, and emits access_granted.
- short-chain-rule: a person with no casting, character or role is a LEGAL state, not a fault. The chain ends early and yields the EMPTY capability set: detected, named upstream, denied everything, with no special case and no new error path (David, 2026-08-16).
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-SENSOR-HORIZON] The sensor knows only hop 1

- rule: the HAL and its implementations have no concept of person, character, role, capability or module. They deal in slots and scores, and no roster semantics, casting table or RBAC may be pushed into a driver.
- what-this-buys: one fingerprint node serves a hatch, a scoreboard and a who-is-aboard display with no special case in any of them.
- the-tell: a driver constant naming a game role, or a sensor-side map from slot to permission.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-RBAC-SCOPE] Roles are global; physical reachability does the scoping

- ruling: David, 2026-08-15 — there are no per-module role definitions. The XO always holds nuclear authorization; a module that places him planetside simply does not place him near the device that exercises it.
- the-three-axes: WHAT a character may do is role to capabilities, global. WHO is playing is casting, session-scoped. WHERE it can be exercised is physical device placement — and the planned layout has real rooms and compartments, so reachability genuinely enforces it.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-AUTHORIZATION-STATE] Hops three to five resolve from published state, not from a resolver service

- proposal: at cast time the service publishes, as retained state addressed to each device, the set of person_ids currently authorized for the capability that device gates. The device binds to that state and answers access_granted locally.
- why-this-shape: a new fact is state, never a handler. The original left hops 3-5 to an undefined middleware — an implicit central hub. Publishing the resolved authorization as state keeps the decision at the leaf, works with the service down, and makes a re-cast a state republication with no template push, preserving the re-casting guarantee.
- what-it-must-not-carry: characters, roles or the RBAC tables themselves. Only the RESOLVED person set for the one capability the device gates — the minimum the leaf needs to own its behaviour.
- status: draft
- escalation: NEW STRUCTURE, architect ratifies. The original specified no home for hops 3-5; this is the conformant candidate, not a ruling.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

---

## Storage tiers

### [CXC-FPID-TIER-LIBRARY] The library is fingerprint_storage and it is unbounded

- holds: every enrolled person and their templates — the whole population, the source of truth.
- is-a-new-service: ruled (David, 2026-08-15). Not an extension of generic storage, so it can be killed without killing anything else.
- sensor-neutrality: the library stores opaque blobs; it parses nothing and knows no vendor.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-KILL-SWITCH] The service is separate for blast-radius containment

- ruling: David, 2026-08-15 — one process to stop, and stopping it stops every path a template can travel.
- structural-consequence: template_ref must be resolvable only by this service. A generic artifact id would let any storage client fetch a template and silently defeat the switch.
- what-it-is-not: security. The assurance class already says this is not access control; the switch bounds an accident, not an adversary.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-TEMPLATE-REF] The template reference is a service-private locator, without a hash

- form: template_ref, spelled fp://person/ordinal — service-private: it travels as an opaque correlation key and nothing outside fingerprint_storage may turn it into bytes or parse it.
- the-hash-half-is-withdrawn: the ratified form carried template_sha256 as its verifier. The no-hashing reconciliation ruling removes it — a low-end MCU cannot hash templates in reasonable time to defend an edge case. Integrity in transit is the transport's; identity of the SET is the uuid ([CXC-FPID-SET-IDENTITY]).
- escalation: HALF-SUPERSEDED RATIFIED DECISION. David ratified locator-plus-hash; the later no-hashing ruling kills the hash. The locator stands; the reference shape should be re-ratified without it.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-TIER-WORKING-SET] The working set is the game's cast

- ruling: David, 2026-08-16 — one game is one cast of up to ten live players, up to twenty templates, loaded IDENTICALLY into every fingerprint module with no sub-segmentation.
- cache-not-truth: the device tier is a cache. Losing it costs a re-push, never an identity.
- the-bound-is-configuration: ten players and two fingers are today's design values; the device-side hardware bound is whatever capabilities announces. Neither number is a schema constant.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

---

## Templates per person

### [CXC-FPID-TEMPLATE-COUNT] One or two templates, and one is never an error

- rule: two is the game-design default; one is a legitimate enrollment, and no code path may treat a one-template person as incomplete or degraded.
- ordinals-are-positional: the ordinal is a position in a list with no anatomical meaning. Nothing tracks left versus right, no interface labels fingers anatomically, and no logic branches on the ordinal (re-ratified by David, 2026-08-16).
- schema-permits-n: express the relation as a list, never as a field pair, a fixed-size array of two, or a validator that rejects a third. The count is a design parameter the structure must not learn.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

#### [CXC-FPID-SCEN-ONE-TEMPLATE] A one-template person authenticates normally

- when: a person enrolled with a single template is in the pushed cast and presents that finger
- then: the match resolves to the person and downstream behaviour is indistinguishable from a two-template person
- part-of: [CXC-FPID-TEMPLATE-COUNT]

### [CXC-FPID-OVERFLOW] The bound fails loud

- rule: a cast that exceeds a device's announced capacity fails the push visibly — never a silent drop, never a partial success reported as success. The device does not adopt the new set's uuid and its needy heartbeat says so.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

---

## The set, its identity, and its movement

### [CXC-FPID-SET-IDENTITY] Identity is a uuid; completeness is a count; there is no hashing

- ruling: reconciliation, 2026-08-18 — NO HASHING anywhere. A low-end MCU cannot hash up to twenty templates in reasonable time to defend an edge case. roster hashes, template hashes and the hash-based identity audit are all removed.
- identity: template_set_uuid, minted by the service for each distinct desired set. A device holds exactly one, durably, as current_template_set_uuid.
- completeness: entry_count. The device counts what it received and loaded against the declared count and refuses a set that does not tally.
- genuineness: a push of the SAME uuid is idempotent — no work, no reset of anything. Only a genuine uuid change triggers the load and zeroes the attempt counter.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-SYNC-MODE] The declarative set is the primitive; add is sugar

- rule: the roster message declares the COMPLETE desired working set. An add-this-player request is expanded by the service into a new desired set with a new uuid; incremental deltas are forbidden on the wire.
- why: a delta protocol drifts silently — a missed add is invisible forever, while a desired-state publication re-asserts the whole truth every time and a missed one costs one round trip.
- self-healing: a device that missed a publication converges on the next one, and the retained desired set converges it the moment it reconnects ([CXC-FPBUS-REQ-RECONCILIATION]).
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-PUSH-TRIGGER] The push trigger is the start of a game

- rule: pick the module, cast the players, start the game — the push falls out as a consequence, not a chore. Full resync at every game start is the normal rhythm.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-RESYNC-COST] The full resync is cast size times template size

- worked-example: twenty templates at 1536 bytes is 30720 bytes — about a second of pure bus time on classic CAN at 500 kbit, trivial on MQTT or UART. Both terms are announced, neither is a constant of this layer.
- operational-rule: affordable BETWEEN ROUNDS, never mid-scene — during the load window the device is authenticating nobody.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-TEMPLATE-TRANSPORT] Templates may ride the bus, one message per template

- ruling: David, 2026-08-15 — templates flow over ComposeBus like anything else; a separate transfer path would be a second protocol to maintain for no security gain.
- framing: one message per template, never one payload carrying the whole set — a whole set sits on the transport's large-payload alarm threshold, and chunking is a substrate property, never a property of a template.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-VERIFICATION] Convergence is read from the heartbeat, and it is testimony

- mechanism: a healthy configured device announces the uuid of the set it holds on its steady-state heartbeat. The service compares that uuid to the current desired set; mismatch triggers a re-push. Reconciliation is a subscription, not a query — no occupancy read, no stamp read, no audit message exists.
- honest-limit: the uuid is the device's claim about itself. With hashing removed there is no remote proof that slot contents match the library; a device that loaded corrupt bytes but stamped the uuid would testify falsely. Accepted deliberately: the sensor is assurance experiential, the fleet is a game, and the failure is bounded by the next re-push.
- escalation: the prior spec's two-tier verification (continuous testimony plus periodic hash audit) was an architect call never ratified by David, and its evidence tier is unimplementable under the no-hashing ruling. It is CUT, and the honest limit above is the recorded cost. Dissent reopens it as a design problem, not a restoration.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-SLOT-MODEL] Slot numbers are device mechanism and never cross the bus

- rule: which slot holds which template is the DEVICE's business. The push carries persons and templates; the device deletes all and loads all, assigning slots as it goes, and resolves hop 2 from its own map.
- what-this-deletes: the service-owned slot allocator, the explicit-slot enrollment invariant, the slot-grant message, and the uniform fleet index as a MANAGED invariant. Loading the same ordered set into an emptied store yields the same layout everywhere anyway — uniformity becomes an emergent property instead of a maintained one, with nothing left that needs to compare slots across devices.
- escalation: RULED-BUT-OBVIATED. The uniform index was adopted on David's proposal, and its stated payoff — divergence made diff-detectable — was consumed entirely by the remote occupancy and audit machinery the semantic contract removes. Porting the slot bureaucracy without its consumer would publish mechanism; flagged for the architect rather than silently dropped.
- what-survives-below: the R503's packaged auto-assign mode is still never used — the general instruction set ruling stands on ring-ownership grounds ([CXC-FPID-ENROLLMENT-PATH]).
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

---

## Two vocabularies and the designed deception

### [CXC-FPID-VOCABULARIES] What happened and what the user is told are different questions

- sensor-outcomes: matched(person), no_match, error — a statement of fact about a finger, consumed by the device and upstream, never by the ring.
- presentation: ready, accept, reject_silent, reject_known, retry, busy — an instruction about what to show, commanded by the device, never a report.
- separation-invariant: the two vocabularies MUST NOT share a token. A shared token invites wiring the sensor result straight to the ring, which type-checks, looks correct, and silently breaks the deception in the one case that matters.
- the-divergent-case: a known person matches and fails the capability check. The sensor says matched; the ring shows unrecognised. Both are correct at their own layer.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-REJECT-SILENT-RENDERING] reject_silent renders identically to a genuine no_match

- rule: whatever a realization does for a genuine non-match, reject_silent does EXACTLY that — same colour, mode, duration and timing. The two stay distinct semantic values above the seam and converge below it.
- why: an observant player who notices a silent rejection blink differently has learned the tell, and the deception is worthless fleet-wide from that moment.
- where-it-binds: on the realization table — [CXC-R503-RING-REALIZATION] for the R503.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-DECEPTION-INTENT] The deception is deliberate

- statement: a device may show unrecognised to a person the sensor recognised. This is designed — the fiction is the product. Do not file it as a defect and do not fix it.
- the-record-is-not-deceived: upstream, user_detected names the person and access_granted records the denial. Only the person at the device is deceived.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

---

## Messages and their address

### [CXC-FPID-UPSTREAM-MESSAGES] The upstream record is two messages

- ruling: David, 2026-08-15 — the device's upstream vocabulary is user_detected carrying a person_id, and access_granted carrying a boolean. Nothing else.
- user-detected: the sensor node's message; asserts identity, never permission; fires on denial too, which makes it the last-seen signal retention needs.
- access-granted: the integrating device's message, never the sensor's — a decision downstream of casting, role, capability and that device's own policy.
- derive-do-not-carry: capability and location are attributes of the device, derivable from the registry by join. Carrying them in a payload duplicates the registry onto the wire, and a duplicate can disagree with its source.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-MSG-ADDRESS] The event carries the device and the minimum key rule

- ruling: David, 2026-08-16 — events are addressed by device: device_id/user_detected, device_id/access_granted. location is a nullable registry attribute and is not in the event vocabulary at all.
- generalisable-rule: a message carries the minimum key needed to identify its source or target; everything else about that party is an attribute resolved by join.
- display-degrades-to-the-key: rendering falls back from Boomer rejected at CIC to Boomer rejected at nuke_console when location is unset. The device id is always the fallback.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-EMIT-SCOPE] The device always publishes locally; the uplink is what is configurable

- rule: every message is published on the device's internal path unconditionally; what is configurable is the UPLINK to the shared bus, default-deny outward, declared by naming the namespace in the peripheral declaration — omission is the off switch, and no new config key exists.
- consequence: retention reads the local or service path and survives an uplink being off; enrollment progress is internal-bus traffic unless an outside peer is deliberately given it.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-FEEDBACK-STATES] The ring is addressed in semantic states

- rule: upstream commands a SEMANTIC state; nobody above the HAL knows a colour exists. A different sensor may have three colours, a buzzer, or nothing — announced via capabilities, so an unshowable state is a known fact rather than an assumption.
- state-set: idle, ready, awaiting_finger, capture_accepted, capture_retry, enroll_progress, busy, fault, plus the presentation values. ready means armed and expecting a finger, distinct from idle.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-RING-OWNER] The device owns the ring always

- ruling: David, 2026-08-16 — the device software always controls the ring, even mid-enrollment. One writer, no arbitration, no handoff.
- consequence-for-the-driver: the HAL publishes semantic progress upward — capture_accepted, capture_retry, progress within the ceremony — and originates no feedback ever. Reporting is not driving.
- why-it-is-structural: with no path from driver to ring, the deception cannot leak through a helpfully-lit green; the guarantee stops resting on discipline.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

---

## Enrollment

### [CXC-FPID-ENROLLMENT-CEREMONY] Enrollment is a ceremony with a real UX cost

- claim: several presentations per template and up to two templates per person — a ten-player cast is on the order of a hundred presses. Enrolling a cast is a scheduled ceremony, not an incidental setup step, and per-press feedback is what makes the ring load-bearing rather than decorative.
- captures-per-template: announced via capabilities and chosen by us under the general instruction set, never inherited as a vendor constant.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-ENROLLMENT-PATH] The general instruction set, never a packaged auto-enroll

- ruling: David, 2026-08-15, ratified. The enrollment path is capture, generate, register, store — never a vendor's packaged command.
- reason-ring: a packaged command drives the ring from inside the module, which is unimplementable under [CXC-FPID-RING-OWNER]. This reason alone rejects it.
- reason-prior-art: no surveyed driver implements the packaged command's ack stream; the general path has prior art in all six.
- reason-instrumentation: the general path exposes the capture count, so progress is reportable and the count is announced.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-ENROLLMENT-HARVEST] Enrollment produces a template for the library

- claim: an enrollment ceremony runs on one station-attached device and ends with the new template READ BACK from that sensor and delivered to fingerprint_storage. The library is the destination of every enrollment; the sensor's copy is scratch until the next push distributes the cast.
- why-this-framing: enroll-everywhere is achieved by PUSH, not by repeating ceremonies per device. The slot used during the ceremony is device-internal mechanism ([CXC-FPID-SLOT-MODEL]).
- capability-dependency: harvest requires template readback. A sensor announcing supports_readback false cannot serve as an enrollment station, and the station learns that from the announce instead of failing silently.
- escalation: REFRAMING. The prior specs justified readback by the now-deleted hash audit; its actual load-bearing consumer is harvest. Architect confirms.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

#### [CXC-FPID-SCEN-ENROLL-HARVEST] A completed ceremony lands in the library

- when: an enrollment ceremony reaches its stored terminal phase on the station's device
- then: the template bytes and their person and ordinal are delivered to fingerprint_storage and the library holds them
- and-then: the next push of a cast containing that person carries the new template to every device
- part-of: [CXC-FPID-ENROLLMENT-HARVEST]

---

## Editor, station, retention

### [CXC-FPID-EDITOR-BOUNDARY] The editor is a client of the service, never its owner

- ruling: David, 2026-08-15. Every operation the editor performs exists as a bus operation the editor merely calls — create person, enroll, define character, role and capability, register a device, cast, push, delete.
- why: a GUI-only operation cannot be scripted, restored headlessly, or driven by an Intelligence, and a second surface would have to reimplement it.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-STATION-SPLIT] Editor and enrollment station are two processes

- ruling: David, 2026-08-16 — two processes, so an Intelligence can drive one end. The seam between them is an API, not an ergonomic split; an Intelligence is just another client of it.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-MANUAL-DELETE] Manual delete is a service operation with a confirmation

- rule: a manual delete is a fingerprint_storage operation with an editor affordance, requiring confirmation, and it triggers a roster republication because the desired set changed.
- open: whether a manual delete inherits the total-delete semantics of the retention ruling is not ruled and must not be inherited by default.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-RETENTION] Six months of inactivity totally deletes

- ruling: David, 2026-08-16 — totally delete both the templates and the person record. Bytes removed, no tombstone.
- the-signal: ANY match resets the six-month clock — user_detected, denied or granted. The target is dormant data, not unprivileged players.
- scope: the SERVICE only. A deleted person is absent from the next cast, so devices converge at the next push with no device-side delete path.
- accepted-cost: historical attempt records carry a person_id that resolves to nothing. Stated in advance; what a consumer renders for it is an open sub-question, and no mitigation is invented here.
- testability: the service takes its clock as an injectable dependency, so a six-month expiry is a unit test rather than a six-month wait.
- device-side-retention: still PARKED — whether a device's flash may keep a deleted person's template until the next push overwrites it is not reopened here.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

#### [CXC-FPID-SCEN-RETENTION-RESET] A denied but active player does not age out

- given: a person whose every interaction is a denial under the deception design
- when: they present a finger within any six-month window
- then: the deletion clock resets, because user_detected fires on denial too
- part-of: [CXC-FPID-RETENTION]

---

## Evidence ledger

### [CXC-FPID-EV-INDEX] The ledger — 0 of 3 slots filled

- slots-total: 3
- slots-filled: 0
- status-of-the-layer: no service, no station and no editor exists; nothing here has run. An unfilled slot carries an explicit EMPTY sentinel.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-FPID]

### [CXC-FPID-EV-01] Re-cast moves no template

- question: re-cast a person to a different character mid-campaign and observe bus traffic.
- expected: the casting table and the authorization state change; no template message and no push occurs.
- discriminates: any template traffic on a re-cast means the model leaked a template dependency into casting.
- observed: EMPTY
- status: draft
- part-of: [CXC-FPID-EV-INDEX]

### [CXC-FPID-EV-02] The short chain denies with no error path

- question: enroll a person, cast nobody, present their finger at a device.
- expected: user_detected names them, access_granted is false, no fault is raised anywhere.
- discriminates: a fault or a special case means the empty-capability-set rule was not implemented as the normal path.
- observed: EMPTY
- status: draft
